WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.980
[Music]

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.819
[Doctor:] ...ask do we have any
guidelines for the future

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:28.359
[Narrator:] This man has acute myelogenous leukemia.

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:30.619
He has flown from his home in Denver

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:34.440
to the U.S. Public Health Service Hospital  in Baltimore,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.660
for new treatment with drugs.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.620
[Patient:] I didn't realize this but I was
interested to know

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.289
so that I could do some personal planning.

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:44.769
[Doctor:] Yes.

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:46.489
[Patient:] Along these lines..

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.219
[Doctor:] I think this is very important for
you to take care of your own,

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.179
make sure your house is in order.

00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:01.960
[Patient:] Sure, umm I was wondering what
the possibilities of a remission

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.430
would be with the drugs which you have mentioned

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.930
and how long this remission would last.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.820
[Doctor:] With our present experience, we are getting remissions at

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:22.150
somewhat over one-third, forty percent of patients.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.800
It is true that when you do reach remission status

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.630
you are clinically and hematologically normal,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.200
there's no evidence of leukemia whatsoever.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.350
Now, our big problem is we are just stymied

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:42.170
and are unable to maintain the remission too long.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:47.970
Exactly from case to case, to predict how long it will last,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.210
I can't do.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.040
We do have some patients that have been in remission well over a year,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.630
a year and a half, two years.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.930
[Patient:] That's a substantial time.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.030
[Doctor:] But there are many many patients

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.640
who were only in remission one or two months.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.630
But of course, let's say we do use daunomycin,

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:15.030
we do get a remission and in the event that
relapse does occur,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.630
we can use cytosine arabinoside.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.400
In fact, we have other drugs after that.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.620
One of the benefits of being here at least,

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.320
is that there are always new things on the
horizon that are here

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.490
if a breakthrough does occur, you have a good
chance of participating

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.140
in that breakthrough here.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.150
[Narrator:] In medical research, there is always
hope for a breakthrough.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.910
Can science find a drug to cure cancer?

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:09.300
[A nurse wheels a patient on a gurney through
the hospital.]

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:14.460
[Narrator:] Cancer has been treated for some
time.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.810
Many of the cancers which form solid tumors
can be treated with surgery.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:23.970
If all the cancerous tissue is removed, the
patient is cured.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.940
[Surgeons work on a patient in the operating room.]

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.500
[Surgeon:] Let's have another [?] please.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.380
[Narrator:] The same is true of radiation
treatments.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:39.700
Cure depends upon destruction of all the cancerous
tissue.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.000
But sometimes the cancer spreads to other
parts of the body.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.370
Leukemia, or cancer of the blood, is of course
all over the body.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.569
In such cases the ideal therapy would be a
chemical

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.180
which could spread through the body and kill
every cancer cell.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.600
[People are shown walking into the National Institutes of Health Clinical Center.]

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.940
[Narrator:] For more than a decade there has
been a national program

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:09.790
to find and test anti-cancer drugs, administered and supported

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.060
in great part by the National Cancer Institute at Bethesda, Maryland.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:18.840
The search is worldwide.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.460
There are ten million natural products that
might contain an anti-cancer compound.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:30.270
Near Nairobi, botanists gather leaves and the bark of trees to process and test against cancer.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.260
[Botanists gather plant material.]

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.740
[A man swims using a snorkel.]

00:04:41.749 --> 00:04:45.159
[Narrator:] In the shallow waters of the Caribbean,

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:48.879
marine biologists collect invertebrates like
the west Indian starfish.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.840
[Marine biologist lifts the starfish out of
the water.]

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.040
[Narrator:] As part of the nationwide cancer
chemotherapy program,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.439
they process and test the starfish to find
out whether it might

00:05:04.439 --> 00:05:10.900
contain drug materials effective against leukemias
and solid tumors.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.339
The search goes on in North Carolina,

00:05:13.339 --> 00:05:15.509
with the gathering of plants and grasses

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:18.649
by botanists with the United States Department
of Agriculture.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:24.300
[The botanist digs plants out of the soil.]

00:05:24.309 --> 00:05:31.109
[Narrator:] Any of these could contain a valuable
anti-cancer agent.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.240
To test these materials for anti-cancer activity,

00:05:36.249 --> 00:05:40.029
researchers might screen them in one thousand
animal tumors...

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:45.080
against one hundred different human cancers.

00:05:45.089 --> 00:05:49.249
How can scientists determine what chemicals
are in these dried twigs?

00:05:49.249 --> 00:05:54.839
[Scientist prepares twigs to be crushed and analyzed.]

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:04.979
[Narrator:] How can such chemicals be tested
safely in human beings with cancer?

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:07.659
The unknown material sent in from the field

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:11.949
is first processed and tested for anti-cancer
capability

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:15.270
in two so-called animal models:

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:24.059
laboratory mice, with a known leukemia, and
rats with a known tumor.

00:06:24.059 --> 00:06:25.009
[Scientist injects animals.]

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:32.909
[Narrator:] These two types of cancer catch
almost all actives.

00:06:32.909 --> 00:06:37.849
If the compound is an active, it will reduce
the tumor mass,

00:06:37.849 --> 00:06:40.919
as compared with the tumor in a rat that got
no compound.

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:45.619
[The tumors are placed side by side to compare the effects of the two treatments.]

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.180
[Narrator:] But if the compound kills tumor cells in a rat,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.580
it is likely to kill some normal cells too.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:55.360
To measure these adverse effects,

00:06:55.369 --> 00:06:58.389
researchers administer the compound to monkeys.

00:06:58.389 --> 00:07:01.430
[A monkey is injected.]

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:06.069
[Narrator:] After a suitable period, they remove
a sample of the monkey's blood

00:07:06.069 --> 00:07:09.089
and subject it to a series of tests that reveal

00:07:09.089 --> 00:07:12.610
the effects of the unknown chemical on normal
blood cells.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.840
For example, the coagulation time.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.140
[Doctors use a stopwatch to monitor the coagulation
time of the monkey's blood.]

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.900
[Narrator:] Other tests show other effects
on the blood,

00:07:24.909 --> 00:07:29.199
and on the kidney, the liver and spleen,

00:07:29.199 --> 00:07:31.389
and particularly on the bone marrow

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:35.759
where red corpuscles and white blood cells
are manufactured.

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:38.649
Such tests indicate the chemicals' toxicity

00:07:38.649 --> 00:07:43.389
and help define a safe dose for the test
in human beings.

00:07:44.289 --> 00:07:47.589
If a compound shows anti-cancer activity,

00:07:47.589 --> 00:07:51.860
other researchers try to determine what it
is composed of, by means of

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:56.879
column chromatography and many other tests.

00:07:56.879 --> 00:08:00.219
The chemical compounds flowing down this column,

00:08:00.219 --> 00:08:04.779
stratified in this way because of their unique
molecular properties,

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:09.559
may turn out to be potent anti-cancer agents.

00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:12.240
More likely they will not.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.560
Only about one agent in fifteen thousand tested
has proven effective.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.200
Chemists also attempt to synthesize new anti-cancer agents.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.860
This is a plastic model, enlarged thousands
of times,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:38.509
of a molecule essential to the reproductive
process in all cells.

00:08:38.509 --> 00:08:41.690
To produce one effective drug,

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:45.560
researchers over many months removed one of
the hydrogens

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.490
and put a fluorine in its place,

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:51.209
producing an antagonist that is absorbed by the cell

00:08:51.209 --> 00:08:54.060
as if it were the real thing...

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.870
then destroys it.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.009
In the last test,

00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:00.500
a safe and effective dose for human beings

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.110
is determined by preclinical pharmacologists.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.940
[Researchers in white lab coats enter a meeting
room.]

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.360
[Narrator:] The moment of truth in the long
search is the clinical trial.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.000
Will the leaves and grasses from Nairobi,

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.300
or the compound synthesized in a laboratory,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:27.500
make a drug to control cancer in human beings?

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:35.360
This is a regular staff conference in the
Clinical Center at Bethesda.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.180
Case histories of patients on trial programs
are presented.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.040
In this instance, the first patient on a new
drug.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.110
Then the doctors discuss the implications
of their findings

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:48.790
for the nationwide research program.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:53.190
[Muffled noise from doctors chatting in the
background.]

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:57.459
[Narrator:] A doctor wonders where the first
phase of drug study should be done.

00:09:57.459 --> 00:09:59.930
[Clinical Doctor 1:] In other words, maybe
the phase one study

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.650
should be done on patients with diseases in which there is

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.300
no established, accepted therapy that would benefit
them.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.300
[Clinical Doctor 2:] Suppose you get phase
one information, say on a solid tumor,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:13.440
and you get some idea what the toxicity,

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:17.740
but you really, is that going to apply in
the face of an acute leukemia, CML

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.020
[?] and I, I think..

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.480
[Clinical Doctor 1:] Well, at least you would
have evaluated the drug

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.920
under uh, relatively clean conditions.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.220
[Clinical Doctor 3:] Yeah but I think the
problem...

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.859
[Clinical Doctor 1:] Where it gets complicated
I think,

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.459
when it involves the marrow, the disease involves
the marrow.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.400
[Narrator:] Much research is being done on
the size of the dose.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:41.050
Large single doses kill cancer cells effectively,

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:45.470
but they also kill some normal cells, so doctors
must balance dosage

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:47.500
against adverse side effects.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.540
[Clinical Doctor 2:] Chuck, we've had one patient who's been on eight hundred,

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.240
which is twice this dose.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.880
[Chuck:] And what's happened to him?

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:00.240
[Clinical Doctor 4:] Uh, he tolerated the
single dose of eight hundred milligrams [?] quite well.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.320
He left the hospital.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:07.040
The second set of liver function tests are
going to be done this week.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.750
Uh, but uh we haven't heard from him.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.350
I venture he's doing quite well.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.860
[Narrator:] Combinations of four drugs are
effective,

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:19.319
but it has been difficult to follow up certain
four-drug programs

00:11:19.319 --> 00:11:22.750
with some of the new drugs, such as cytosine
arabinoside.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.580
[Clinical Doctor 1:] I think this brings up
an important point

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.960
about where phase one studies should be done,

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:33.740
because we noted when we first started using
cytosine arabinoside,

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.740
it was after the four-drug combination, after
people relapsed,

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:42.840
and we saw a significant number of patients
with uh,

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:48.940
often quite severe lymphatic damage with cytosine
arabinoside.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.879
And we were very concerned about it.

00:11:51.879 --> 00:11:57.050
Other institutions were starting right off
with cytosine arabinoside, and you know,

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:01.640
and so I think it's probably for the same
reason.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:05.220
The people we had had been in remission an
average of nine or ten months.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:10.269
And uh, the time they got the cytosine arabinoside
they were just more sensitive to drugs.

00:12:10.269 --> 00:12:14.540
Same thing applies to re-induction with the
four-drug combination.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:17.980
We saw a higher degree of lymphatic dysfunction
in re-induction with the same drugs

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.339
than we did in primary induction

00:12:20.339 --> 00:12:22.699
when the patient was first diagnosed.

00:12:22.699 --> 00:12:25.829
[Muffled noise in the background; doctors
chatting again.]

00:12:25.829 --> 00:12:29.060
[Narrator:] Some cancers adversely affect
the bone marrow,

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:33.509
where red corpuscles and white blood cells
are produced.

00:12:33.509 --> 00:12:36.279
By studying a sample of the bone marrow,

00:12:36.279 --> 00:12:38.819
doctors can follow the evolution of the disease

00:12:38.819 --> 00:12:41.129
and the effects of different drug programs.

00:12:41.129 --> 00:12:43.989
[Clinical Doctor 2:] What does the present
bone marrow show?

00:12:43.989 --> 00:12:45.129
I mean, did we get our own marrow

00:12:45.129 --> 00:12:47.869
or are we relying on the marrow from cytogenetics?

00:12:47.869 --> 00:12:50.809
[Off-screen Doctor:] No, we have not obtained
the marrow yet.

00:12:50.809 --> 00:12:53.069
[Female Doctor:] He hasn't had one yet here,

00:12:53.069 --> 00:12:55.249
but he had [?] rods in the peripheral blood

00:12:55.249 --> 00:12:56.360
so we're not in any doubt about the diagnosis.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.380
[Off-screen Doctor:] But the percent blasts
are only five percent.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:04.800
[Narrator:] To take a marrow sample, the doctor
carefully anesthetizes the local area,

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.639
then inserts the needle through the flesh
into the end of the bone.

00:13:09.639 --> 00:13:12.120
[A needle is inserted into the bone of the
patient.]

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:20.759
[The needle is forced further down until it
reaches the bone marrow.]

00:13:20.759 --> 00:13:26.750
[Narrator:] And on into the marrow.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:39.140
A syringe withdraws a small sample of the
marrow.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:47.120
This is placed on microscope slides, then
examined.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:14:11.120
[The slides are prepared and placed under a microscope., which the scientist adjusts.]

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:18.780
[Narrator:] This marrow sample from a patient
with acute leukemia is hypercellular,

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:22.740
saturated with malignant cells.

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.839
At a larger magnification the malignant cells,

00:14:25.839 --> 00:14:31.480
called blasts, show an unusually large nucleus.

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.810
After two courses of treatment with drugs,

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.670
a marrow sample might show fewer blasts

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:41.860
and increasing numbers of normal white cells,
a sign of improvement.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:45.939
Later, in remission, when the disease is under
control,

00:14:45.939 --> 00:14:53.540
there will be only five percent blasts.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.199
But the peripheral blood, the blood that is
flowing through the body,

00:14:57.199 --> 00:14:59.819
is also a good indicator of the state of the

00:14:59.819 --> 00:15:02.480
patient's disease and the efficacy of treatment.

00:15:04.939 --> 00:15:14.449
[Narrator:] A sample of the patient's blood
is diluted and placed on a counter.

00:15:14.449 --> 00:15:18.449
The dials reveal the number of blood cells
in a measured sample.

00:15:18.449 --> 00:15:24.399
A symptom of leukemia is an excessive number
of abnormal white blood cells,

00:15:24.399 --> 00:15:30.139
ineffective against invading micro-organisms.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.709
Cancer research needs many blood donors.

00:15:35.709 --> 00:15:45.379
[A woman sits in a chair, having her blood drawn by a medical worker.]

00:15:45.379 --> 00:15:51.389
[Narrator:] Leukemia, for example, sometimes
reduces the number of blood platelets,

00:15:51.389 --> 00:15:55.879
the tiny cells that maintain the integrity
of blood vessel walls,

00:15:55.879 --> 00:15:57.769
Bleeding may result.

00:15:57.769 --> 00:16:02.490
But, platelets can be replaced by transfusion.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.390
Whole blood is removed,

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:08.029
centrifuged to bring down the heavier red
cells,

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:12.999
and placed on an extractor that squeezes the platelet-rich plasma

00:16:12.999 --> 00:16:18.180
off the top of the sample, for transfusion into
the patient--

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:23.160
in this case, the son of the donor.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:32.660
Since both the drug and the cancer may affect
the body's ability

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:36.009
to resist invading micro-organisms,

00:16:36.009 --> 00:16:38.089
many patients die of infection.

00:16:38.089 --> 00:16:42.360
For protection, some patients are placed in
a plastic bubble

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:47.740
that completely surrounds them at all times.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.660
Doctors conduct routine examinations

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:55.699
even though they are completely cut off from
the patient.

00:16:55.699 --> 00:17:00.870
Only filtered air and aseptic materials enter
this sterile, private world.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:05.320
Yet doctors can perform bone marrow and other
operations.

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:13.000
[Doctor extracts bone marrow from the quarantined
patient.]

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:20.120
[Narrator:] This laminar flow room now under
test at Bethesda

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:24.710
may someday replace the plastic bubble.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:28.220
It will repel micro-organisms by continually
blowing

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:32.440
fresh, filtered air across the patient.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:38.550
Doctors and visitors will enter and remain
downstream

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:43.520
If the experimental drug cannot control the
number of white blood cells,

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:46.960
doctors use the continuous-flow blood cell
separator,

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:51.500
shown at the M.D. Anderson Hospital and Tumor
Institute in Houston.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.420
The machine draws whole blood from one arm,

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:57.720
pumps it through carefully sterilized tubes
to a weight-sensitive

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:00.490
buffer bag which controls the pump,

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:05.240
and sends it to a centrifuge which is similar
to a cream separator.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.240
Because of the different densities of white
cells, red cells,

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:10.010
and other blood elements,

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.840
they separate at different points along the
radius of the disk.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:19.190
Unwanted cells are shunted aside and the rest
returned to the patient.

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:28.960
[Chuck:] This is an important point...

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:33.940
[Narrator:] If a drug is successful against
one kind of cancer in phase one,

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.030
it is tested against other kinds.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:41.090
Then it is sent for further tests to many
hospitals around the country.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:47.100
[Clinical Doctor 2:] Does anyone know what the liver toxicity has been with the first end result?

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:53.620
Since that's been tried pretty extensively now in the midwest.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:57.320
[Clinical Doctor 1:] There has not really
been much mention made of

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.600
liver toxicity in the patients that have been
evaluated

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:02.580
after therapy, so I would assume,

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:05.840
although I don't know directly, that we haven't
used the old drug here.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.120
That there really isn't a significant amount
of liver toxicity

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.920
with that particular kind of thing.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:13.150
[Narrator:] Always there are the questions
of scheduling.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:15.880
Balancing dosage against toxicity.

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:19.400
[Clinical Doctor 1:] We ought to really be
careful about how we evaluate and look at toxicity...

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:20.500
[Clinical Doctor 2:] No

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:23.600
[Clinical Doctor 1:] in the circumstances
that occur so we don't do this all over again.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.730
[Clinical Doctor 2:] I'll agree with you very
much on that.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:30.800
The thing is, one of the reasons that we are
happy with this

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.140
type of schedule; that is, giving one dose,

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.080
and then waiting to see recovery,

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:39.620
is that it gives us time to back off and let
things uh,

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:41.480
let the toxicity abate,

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:45.320
and I think it's probably the safest way to
start out giving a drug.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:50.520
[Narrator:] And finally the question of an
adequate trial for each drug.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:54.620
[Clinical Doctor 3:] What would you consider
an adequate trial for this drug,

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:57.480
how much and how long?

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:00.020
[Clinical Doctor 1:] In this particular patient?

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:05.680
[Clinical Doctor 3:] Yes, how long?

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:09.220
[Clinical Doctor 1:] Well, I think that the,
I would think that

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:10.680
in this particular patient,

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.480
if the physicians in charge think it's due
to liver,

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:17.060
then I suspect...liver toxicity is due to
the drug...

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:19.440
then you can say well, we have this stuff
because of liver toxicity,

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:25.490
but my only question is I'm not so sure we
can attribute it all to the drug

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:29.860
and would feel that the risk involved in giving
him, say, a half-dose

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:31.380
would not be that great.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:35.480
It would still allow us to see if this drug
is indeed going to be useful.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:40.920
And we're hoping that uh, this drug would
be useful in this particular instance.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:45.400
[Narrator:] In clinical research the doctor
is concerned

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:49.940
not just with a drug and an effective schedule
of administration,

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:52.650
but with a whole patient and his disease.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:54.700
[Doctor:] You've given her daunomycin how long?

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.000
[Doctor 2:] She's gotten one dose.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:56.880
[Doctor:] One dose.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:59.270
[Doctor 2:] Exactly one week ago and she is
scheduled for her second dose today.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:01.590
[Doctor:] Have you ever had any trouble with
your heart?

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.030
[Patient:] Not that I know of.

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:04.900
[Doctor:] Never had a murmur as a child?

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:06.460
[Patient:] Not that I know of.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.460
[Doctor:] Have you found anything?

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:13.460
[Doctor 2:] Her cardiac status is completely
normal as far as we can tell.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:17.780
[Narrator:] But the doctor always returns
to the key questions.

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:22.190
Which dosage has worked? Which schedule has
been most effective?

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:26.090
[Doctor 2:] A level of sixteen thousand or
twenty-five thousand,

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:30.650
but in terms of concentration, it's not too
different.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.810
[Doctor:] She didn't respond at all to actinomycin?

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:38.500
[Doctor 2:] Ahhh, on one occasion her titer became completely negative,

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:42.970
that is zero, and subsequently came back up
again, though.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:44.490
This was on actinomycin day.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.130
[Doctor 2:] In the last few months she's received

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:52.510
three or four courses of methotrexate infusion
type and her titers have

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:56.740
gone down and back up, but her general course has remained essentially unchanged.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:22:01.390
[Doctor 2:] In the light of the natural course of this disease

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:04.040
it's fair to say the methotrexate has been
somewhat effective.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.650
[Doctor:] Did you ever decide in your mind
whether

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:11.360
she was really drug-resistant or just perhaps a dosage...schedule...

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:13.850
[Doctor 2:] I think it's difficult to say.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:17.670
If we could give the methotrexate more often,
perhaps we might have a better effect.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:18.680
[Doctor:] What's been the limiting factor...

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.260
[Doctor 2:] The limiting factor has been um,
toxicity

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:27.380
related primarily to the ulcerations in her mouth and uh,

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:33.530
which have been quite severe, although not complicated by infection so far.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:36.340
[Doctor:] She had ulcerations anywhere else
with methotrexate?

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:37.920
[Doctor 2:] No, not that we know of.

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:38.770
[Patient:] A rash.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:42.520
[Doctor 2:] She did have a rash on her skin,

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:46.080
which I couldn't say was clearly related to
methotrexate, but may have been.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:48.130
She has not had it with each dose.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:52.640
[Doctor 2:] She's tolerated therapy actually
quite well.

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:56.120
[Doctor:] Did you notice anything when you
got your first dose of daunomycin?

00:22:56.120 --> 00:22:58.510
[Patient:] No, I was really surprised.

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.400
[Doctor:] Pleasantly surprised?

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.420
[Patient:] Yes, very much so.

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:06.080
[Doctor:] Well, it's going to be a long dosage
schedule.

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:07.780
[Patient:] Okay.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:13.420
[Doctor:] We've had some good luck with it,
as Dr. Nigel mentioned.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:18.460
[Scene switches to a different patient's room.]

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:20.940
[Doctor 1:] Good morning.

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:21.700
[Mr. Simpson:] Good morning.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:23.140
[Doctor 1:] How are you doing today?

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:26.480
[Narrator:] The guiding principal in all good
clinical research

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.130
is concern for the patient.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.400
[Doctor 2:] That's not a worry, we don't, we're not quite as relaxed as you are.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.920
[Patient:] Boy, I'm not relaxed.

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:37.540
[Doctor 2:] How's the back doing today, and
the legs?

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.260
[Mr. Simpson:] My legs hurt.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:40.660
[Doctor 2:] They do hurt.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:45.180
[Mr. Simpson:] Down here yeah. My back don't
bother me, hasn't for a long time.

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:49.600
[Doctor 2:] I wonder if you could roll over
on your side,

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:52.800
and roll towards the window. We just want to feel your back.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:24:01.140
[Patient turns toward the window as instructed and doctor places his hand on the man's back.]

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.740
[Doctor 2:] I want you to tell me, Mr. Simpson if this uh,

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:11.460
when and if this hurts you at all.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.520
That tender?

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:15.940
[Mr. Simpson:] No, not yet.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:16.980
[Doctor 2:] That tender?

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:18.320
[Mr. Simpson:] Nope.

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.240
[Doctor 2:] That tender?

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.480
[Mr. Simpson:] That's a little bit.

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.100
[Doctor 2:] How 'bout over here?

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:26.420
[Mr. Simpson:] Nope.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.000
[Doctor 2:] Over here?

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:29.300
[Mr. Simpson:] No.

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:31.060
[Doctor 2:] Right here?

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:35.770
[Mr. Simpson:] Well, I believe that's a little
bit right there.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:36.350
[Doctor 2:] Here?

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:37.410
[Mr. Simpson:] Nope.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:39.360
[Doctor 2:] Here?

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:46.580
[Mr. Simpson:] No...a little bit right there. That's it at the end of your finger.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:51.600
Right there.

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:52.960
[Doctor 2:] A lot or a little bit?

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:54.980
[Mr. Simpson:] No, just a little.

00:24:57.020 --> 00:25:02.740
[Doctor 3:] I wonder if the [?] would reproduce any of the pain down the leg.

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:15.860
Well, Tom, we finally got the results on the x-rays.

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.360
[Mr. Simpson:] And what'd you get?

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:21.100
[Doctor 3:] And there does seem to be something that

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:25.460
may well be causing the pain down
the leg, but it's in this area.

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:29.080
Uh, we're going to talk with the radiotherapy
people today

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:32.940
and see if they can help us on it.

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:37.580
We'll let your know later on today on that.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:40.600
[Mr. Simpson:] You'll let me know today.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:43.680
[Doctor 3:] I think so, it'll depend on when
they will be able to get to see you.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:47.540
Your next course of chemotherapy starts next
week.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.440
[Mr. Simpson:] Next week?

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.440
[Doctor 3:] Yeah, Tuesday..

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:53.660
[Doctors 1 and 3:] Monday or Tuesday

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.330
[Doctor 3:] Depending on what your blood count
shows.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:59.840
They think they can help you if they combine
the two.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.120
[Mr. Simpson:] Okay.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:05.820
[Doctor 3:] Okay so maybe that's, that's what's
doing it.

00:26:05.820 --> 00:26:07.060
[Mr. Simpson:] You think so?

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:07.950
[Doctor 3:] Yeah, I think.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.800
[Doctor 2:] Pretty good chance, uh-huh.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:11.440
[Mr. Simpson:] I think that sounds right.

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:13.310
[Doctor 2:] Well everything else, you know,
has improved.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.200
Lump has gone down, chest is better and so
forth.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.520
[Doctor 2:] So if we can get this pain under
control I think uh,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.420
everything should be a lot better, Mr. Simpson.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:26.680
[Mr. Simpson:] Okay.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:28.320
[Doctor 2:] So that's what we're shooting
for.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:29.020
[Mr. Simpson:] All right.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:29.540
[Doctor 2:] Okay.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:36.660
[Narrator:] Where has the National Cancer
Institute program led?

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:39.900
In advanced forms of Hodgkin's disease,

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.740
a four-drug treatment produced complete remissions,

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:47.280
or temporary disappearance of all evidence of cancer in twice as many patients

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:50.200
as ordinarily achieved remission with single
drugs.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:56.490
A group of thirty-five children with acute
leukemia were treated

00:26:56.490 --> 00:27:00.020
intermittedly with massive doses of four drugs.

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:03.450
The median survival of these children was
three years

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.480
compared with a three-month survival of leukemic
children

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:10.780
twenty years ago.

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:14.620
The story of cancer drug research will continue
next week

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:18.310
in part two, "The Battle in the Cell."

00:27:28.860 --> 00:28:25.840
[Music]

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:33.980
[Narrator:] For further information on Cancer chemotherapy, write for a free booklet,

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:38.860
Drugs versus Cancer, available from the Research Information Branch,

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:42.780
National Cancer Institute, Bethesda, Maryland.

00:28:46.280 --> 00:29:16.380
[Music]

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:23.780
[Narrator:] These are normal human cells from prostatic tissue

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:25.370
living in a laboratory dish.

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:27.660
They were filmed through a microscope

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:33.780
that magnifies them two-hundred seventy times.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:38.940
These are the same kind of human cells from
prostatic tissue,

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:41.680
grown and filmed in the same way,

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:44.820
but they are cancer cells.

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:50.760
The cells, normal and cancer, differ in ways
not yet fully understood.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.600
It is the task of the cancer chemotherapist,

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:57.760
the scientist who hopes to control cancer
through the use of drugs,

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:02.920
to exploit those differences so that a drug can kill all of these cells,

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:05.960
without killing more than a few of these

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:08.570
in the same human being.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:26.980
[Narrator:] Cancer has been treated successfully for some time

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:30.730
by surgery in which the malignant tissue is removed.

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:43.460
And by radiation in which it is destroyed
by cobalt and x-rays.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:46.280
But sometimes cancer spreads to other parts of the body

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:50.500
even as the local area is being treated successfully.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:56.670
In such cases, new hope may be offered by chemotherapy, the use of drugs.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:31:00.530
[Doctor:] What treatments have you had? I know you had surgery; did you ever have x-ray therapy?

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:01.500
[Patient:] Um-hmm

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:02.880
[Doctor:] When was that?

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:07.480
[Patient:] Sixty-three I think. Couldn't say
for sure.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:11.120
[Doctor:] And that was for a local recurrence
of your disease, I think.

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:12.540
[Patient:] Yes.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:14.880
And then, did that hold it under control for a little while?

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:15.860
[Patient:] Yes, it did.

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:17.480
[Doctor:] And then what happened?

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:21.720
[Patient:] Well then, it started all over
again.

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:24.340
[Doctor:] Have you ever received any other
therapy?

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:27.160
Uhh, very often patients with breast cancer

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:33.060
get estrogen therapy, corticosteroids, cortisone.

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:35.020
[Patient:] The only thing I had was those
pills.

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:38.720
And what are those, I can't pronounce..

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:41.720
[Doctor:] Diethyl silbesterol, that's it.

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:44.420
[Doctor:] These are, are pills, this is hormones

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:49.060
that very often control cancer, breast cancer.

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:50.100
Did this help?

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:55.140
[Patient:] Yes, when I was taking two or three it helped make it better.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:59.060
Then I had to stop because I had started my period.

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:02.720
[Doctor:] Basically, the problem with your disease is that

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:09.660
you did have localized disease and you received surgery

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:13.720
and then for a local recurrence, radiotherapy,

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:18.060
and I think now the problem is that the disease
is just too far out,

00:32:18.070 --> 00:32:20.410
too widespread to be controlled

00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:21.980
by any of these local means.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:25.710
[Doctor:] Where did Dr. Hunter tell you that
you had disease?

00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:29.420
[Patient:] My breast.

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:35.420
[Doctor:] Eventually what happens in cancer
is that we do have to use drugs.

00:32:35.430 --> 00:32:36.790
[Patient:] Mhmm.

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:41.650
[Doctor:] Even after local therapy and after
hormonal therapy.

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:46.430
You did have the hormonal therapy, and the disease has progressed to

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:51.100
the point where your doctor did feel that
you should go to a center

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:56.640
that is more concerned, that has more experience
in drug treatment.

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:57.710
[Patient:] Right.

00:32:57.710 --> 00:32:59.870
[Doctor:] Were you happy about going or were
you,

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:03.020
how did this strike you when you discussed
this with him?

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:06.300
[Patient:] Well, it kind of shocked me a little bit.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:10.740
Then I made up my mind I would, he, he advised
me it was the best thing for me to do.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:17.800
He said that if I could get in here I would be very lucky, so I told him I would.

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:21.440
[Narrator:] For more than a decade, the National Cancer Institute

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:27.560
has guided and supported a large national program of cancer chemotherapy.

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:30.680
Scientists are studying the whole patient,

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:35.010
the effects of many kinds of drugs on the
course of his disease,

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:37.760
and on the inner workings of cancer cells.

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:41.790
A cell is visible to the human eye only through
a microscope.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:46.320
Nevertheless, it is the building block of
which all tissue is made.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.220
When new tissue is needed the cell divides, each so-called mother

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:54.930
producing two daughters exactly like
the mother.

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:59.410
Each daughter produces two more daughters,
also the same.

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:02.750
When no more tissue is needed, the doubling
stops,

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:07.190
halted by an unknown biological signal.

00:34:07.190 --> 00:34:10.870
The cancer cell and all its daughter cells
do not receive

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:13.220
or do not obey the signal.

00:34:13.220 --> 00:34:18.409
They keep multiplying far beyond what the
body can tolerate.

00:34:18.409 --> 00:34:26.040
This relentless growth of nonfunctional cells
can lead to illness and death.

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:29.770
The experimental evidence of this is startling.

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:33.720
Blood is withdrawn from the abdomen of a mouse
with leukemia.

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:41.960
[Scientist uses a syringe to to withdraw blood from the mouse's body.]

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:47.000
[Narrator:] It is diluted.

00:34:47.970 --> 00:34:53.350
And the cells are counted under a microscope.

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:55.730
They are diluted and counted again,

00:34:55.730 --> 00:35:05.400
until any given drop is likely to contain
only one or two leukemic cells.

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:08.540
With this instrument, called a micro-manipulator,

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:11.860
scientists at Southern Research Institute in Birmingham

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:17.270
today can isolate a single living leukemic
cell.

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:39.450
[The scientist assembles his micro-manipulator.]

00:35:40.660 --> 00:35:44.460
[Narrator:] Using a hair-like tube, the technician places several drops

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:53.760
of the diluted material on a glass slide.

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:01.160
The slide is turned over so the drops are hanging

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:05.940
from the lower surface, and is placed under a microscope.

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:12.900
The technician locates a single drop.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:28.720
He carefully moves a pipette, a delicate glass
tube, up under the slide.

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:33.980
And with hydraulically-operated controls that
scale down his movements,

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:39.580
he inserts it into the droplet...

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:56.100
and withdraws the leukemic cell.

00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:10.970
The cell is transferred to a hypodermic syringe.

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:16.930
A single cancer cell.

00:37:16.930 --> 00:37:21.040
It is injected into a normal, healthy mouse.

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:29.300
[A researcher holds a mouse in her hand and injects the cancer cell, then places the mouse in a cage.]

00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:39.120
[Narrator:] Within eight days, there is a
noticeable abdominal swelling,

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:42.930
evidence of the growth of leukemic cells.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:47.690
The cancer cells continue to increase, each
mother producing two daughters.

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:50.270
Each daughter producing two more daughters.

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:55.640
Until, after ten days the swelling in the
abdomen is pronounced.

00:37:55.640 --> 00:38:00.180
By the nineteenth day, the single cancer cell will have proliferated

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:03.140
to more than one billion cells.

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:06.660
Enough to kill the animal.

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:10.600
But certain chemicals can stop this cancerous
growth.

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:15.330
In this laboratory culture, we see mouse leukemia cells dividing,

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:19.650
with cleavage as shown in the center of the screen,

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:26.060
completed after the usual length of time.

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:29.240
But when a drug is added,

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:33.790
the reproductive cycle of the cells is violentlydisrupted.

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:36.920
The cell in the center of the culture stays
at metaphase,

00:38:36.920 --> 00:38:39.720
an intermediate stage in the division process,

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:42.560
an unusually long time.

00:38:42.560 --> 00:38:45.040
Cleavage is irregular.

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:51.520
Daughter cells are of unusual size and do not survive very long.

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:55.700
Human cancer cells are also affected by drugs.

00:38:55.710 --> 00:39:00.060
These cancer cells in a laboratory dish are dividing in the usual way,

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:04.920
as shown at far right.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:10.560
If an alkaloid from the periwinkle plant is added,

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:13.660
cells proceed to the second phase of the cycle,

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:16.060
as seen in the rounded cells,

00:39:16.060 --> 00:39:19.570
but cannot go beyond this phase.

00:39:19.570 --> 00:39:22.220
Mother cells cannot produce daughters,

00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:24.120
stopping the relentless reproduction

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:28.860
that characterizes the growth of cancer cells.

00:39:28.860 --> 00:39:33.820
But it has been found that a given dose of
a given anti-cancer drug

00:39:33.820 --> 00:39:38.760
destroys only a certain percentage of the
cells in a living animal.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.060
Regardless of the absolute number of cancer cells,

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:45.060
the percentage destroyed by the same size dose will

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:47.280
always be the same.

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:52.940
Suppose a given type of cancer cell doubles
approximately every twelve hours.

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:55.820
Once every twenty-four hours we administer
a drug

00:39:55.820 --> 00:40:00.330
which kills sixty percent of the cancer cells
present.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:05.080
Again, the remaining cancer cells double.

00:40:05.080 --> 00:40:08.250
And again, we kill sixty percent.

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:09.830
We have slowed them down,

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:12.490
but they are increasing faster than we are
killing them,

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:15.820
and they will eventually increase in mass
or number

00:40:15.820 --> 00:40:19.780
to the point where they kill the animal.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:22.710
We can increase the doses

00:40:22.710 --> 00:40:27.100
so that we are killing the cancer cells faster
than they can multiply.

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:31.740
But anti-cancer agents also affect normal
cells.

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:33.730
If the dose is too large,

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:38.240
and if too many normal cells are killed while
we are destroying the cancer cells

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:41.420
the animal may sicken and die.

00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:49.220
Is it possible to kill large numbers of cancer
cells with drugs

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:52.050
without killing too many normal cells?

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:04.880
[Narrator:] These normal mice are injected
with an anti-cancer drug called cyclophosphamide.

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:12.400
Twenty-four hours later the femur, or upper
leg bone of the mouse, is removed,

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:14.060
the ends clipped,

00:41:14.180 --> 00:41:21.600
[The scientist uses a tiny instrument to clips the ends of the mouse's femur bone.]

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:26.360
[Narrator:] and the bone marrow, the site
of blood-forming stem cells,

00:41:26.369 --> 00:41:30.990
is washed out and diluted in a laboratory
solution.

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:37.830
[The described procedure is shown.]

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:46.120
[Narrator:] To find out how the drug has affected
the marrow and its ability to produce cells,

00:41:46.130 --> 00:41:49.230
the technician injects the diluted marrow
into a mouse

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:53.170
whose stem cells have been destroyed.

00:41:53.170 --> 00:41:56.270
Ten days later, after the injected marrow
cells

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:58.320
have had a chance to multiply,

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:03.140
the animal's spleen is removed and fixed in
Bouin solution.

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:14.820
[The mouse's spleen is dropped into a test
tube filled with the solution.]

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:23.440
[Narrator:] Marrow cells form nodules on the spleen.

00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:26.810
The number of nodules, in this case a dozen or so,

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:29.880
indicates the number of cells surviving,

00:42:29.890 --> 00:42:36.650
a measure of the effects of the original anti-cancer agent on the normal cell.

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:39.850
But what does the agent do to cancer cells?

00:42:40.640 --> 00:42:43.580
A lymphoma-bearing mouse is injected

00:42:43.590 --> 00:42:49.850
and twenty-four hours later its bone marrow is removed.

00:42:53.530 --> 00:42:57.180
Again following the same procedure used with the normal mouse,

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:01.690
the marrow is diluted and injected into the tail of a mouse,

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:03.920
which will serve as a kind of incubator

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:07.850
or culture dish for the marrow cells.

00:43:07.850 --> 00:43:11.990
Eight days later the spleen of the cancer
mouse is removed.

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:14.260
Again the nodules are counted,

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:16.850
an indication this time of the rate of survival

00:43:16.850 --> 00:43:21.890
of cancer cells after drug treatment.

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:28.730
To get good data, sixty-six such experiments
were conducted

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.270
at the Ontario Cancer Institute,

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:39.510
each one involving more than one hundred mice.

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:45.290
When the results were plotted,

00:43:45.290 --> 00:43:48.630
the curve showing the percent survival of normal cells

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:52.380
as against increasing drug dosage, looked like this.

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:56.100
[Man draws curve on paper indicating relationship
between dosage and cells killed.]

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:59.330
The curve indicating percent survival of cancer cells

00:43:59.330 --> 00:44:04.490
with increased drug dosage looked like this.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:11.520
There is no question that some drugs kill
many more cancer cells

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:15.220
than normal cells in the same living organism.

00:44:15.220 --> 00:44:18.850
And that kill-ratios vary with dosage.

00:44:18.850 --> 00:44:26.250
So, dose amount, as well as timing, is important in the battle in the cell.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.800
But can we further improve this ratio?

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:33.720
The ratio of cancer cell killed to normal
cell killed.

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:35.340
Just within the past couple of years,

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:37.960
a new approach to this problem has been developed,

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:44.020
based upon close observation of the life cycle
of the cell.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:46.960
In addition to the phase during which the
cell divides,

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:49.960
there are several other cycle phases.

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:54.160
Generally, cells appear to rest in one phase,

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:57.960
synthesize genetic material in another,

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.040
rest again, then divide.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:02.140
[A graphic illustrates as narrator describes the process.]

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:04.400
But timing is crucial.

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:11.010
How can we find the length of each phase in
the life cycle of a cancer cell?

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:12.690
At Southern Research Institute,

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:17.260
researchers use a radioactive material that
is absorbed into cancer cells

00:45:17.260 --> 00:45:18.940
during the S phase.

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:30.300
[Scientists move about in the lab, preparing to run a test on the lab mice.]

00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:36.150
They inject it into laboratory mice

00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:40.640
with the solid tumor designated sarcoma 180,

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:43.080
let the tumor grow,

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:47.480
then remove it.

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:52.869
In this tumor, how many cells have taken up
the radioactive material?

00:45:52.869 --> 00:45:57.449
How many have passed through the S phase and
on into mitosis, or cell division?

00:45:57.640 --> 00:46:07.160
[The scientist is shown cutting a tumor in a petri dish into small pieces.]

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:14.660
After mincing the tumor,

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:19.020
the technician suspends it in a measured amount
of saline solution

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:23.020
and carefully breaks down the tumor tissue
in a laboratory homogenizer.

00:46:23.020 --> 00:46:35.420
[A whirring sound is heard as the described process is shown.]

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:45.260
Then the technician strains the mixture

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:55.240
to remove large clumps of tissue,

00:47:03.869 --> 00:47:07.240
and places the liquid containing radioactive
cancer cells

00:47:07.240 --> 00:47:10.840
into a centrifruge.

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:17.020
[The centrifruge begins spinning.]

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:20.500
Later, the packed cells are treated in other ways

00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:23.530
and are placed on glass slides, each carefully marked

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:25.650
for future identification.

00:47:29.200 --> 00:47:39.440
[Carrying the cell sample slides, a scientist approaches and opens a lab door marked "Warning."]

00:47:41.380 --> 00:47:44.359
[Narrator:] To find out how many cells are
radioactive,

00:47:44.359 --> 00:47:47.030
and thus have passed through the S phase,

00:47:47.030 --> 00:47:50.720
the slide is converted into a kind of photographic
plate.

00:47:50.720 --> 00:47:54.250
It is coated with emulsion and left in a dark
room for six weeks.

00:47:54.250 --> 00:48:12.430
[The described process is shown.]

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:26.119
To count the cells that have absorbed the
radioactive material,

00:48:26.119 --> 00:48:28.800
the researcher not only examines all portions

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:31.330
of the slide under a microscope,

00:48:31.330 --> 00:48:35.150
but also studies it on closed-circuit television.

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:38.540
Cells containing radioactive material are identified

00:48:38.550 --> 00:48:43.700
by the dark spots on the emulsion.

00:48:43.700 --> 00:48:51.220
On this slide, over three thousand cells were
studied and counted.

00:48:53.140 --> 00:48:55.260
From this experiment,

00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:59.380
the researchers determined that the life cycle
of the sarcoma 180 cell

00:48:59.380 --> 00:49:01.990
is about thirteen and a half hours,

00:49:01.990 --> 00:49:05.609
and that the S phase lasts almost eight hours,

00:49:05.609 --> 00:49:08.270
important data in determining the best time

00:49:08.270 --> 00:49:14.640
to administer drugs that kill during the S
phase.

00:49:14.640 --> 00:49:21.020
Of the thousands of drugs tested, some apparently
kill only during the S phase of the life cycle.

00:49:23.620 --> 00:49:28.240
Yet their kill ratio is good.

00:49:28.240 --> 00:49:32.840
Of the thousands tested, some were found to
kill throughout the life cycle

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:40.800
and they also can give good cancer-cell to
normal-cell kill ratios.

00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:44.060
But when these different kinds of drugs are
combined,

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:51.369
they can give much better kill ratios.

00:49:51.369 --> 00:49:55.220
Much experimentation involves drug schedules.

00:49:55.220 --> 00:49:57.190
Experimenters at Southern Research Institute

00:49:57.190 --> 00:50:00.050
have developed ways to administer drugs steadily,

00:50:00.050 --> 00:50:08.410
over long periods of time, to mice with different kinds of cancer.

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:20.840
A tube is inserted intraperitoneally

00:50:20.850 --> 00:50:24.140
and the mouse is allowed to run into his small
chamber along with

00:50:24.140 --> 00:50:29.680
a dozen or so of his cousins.

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:36.720
The experimental drug is drawn into a special
syringe.

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:44.980
[The described process is shown.]

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:51.080
And the syringe is mounted on a rack,

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:58.420
one syringe for each mouse.

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:04.980
Other syringes are filled with harmless saline
solution

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:08.790
for administration to a group of control mice.

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:12.310
They will be watched to see whether the apparatus,
rather than the drug,

00:51:12.310 --> 00:51:15.780
affects them in any way.

00:51:15.790 --> 00:51:18.369
Now each syringe is adjusted precisely

00:51:18.369 --> 00:51:22.469
to ensure administration of a carefully measured
amount of drug.

00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:30.760
[The described process is shown.]

00:51:32.600 --> 00:51:36.520
The mice in their stalls are given a final
check,

00:51:36.520 --> 00:51:38.920
a timer is started,

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:43.609
and the drug is fed through the tubes at a constant rate.

00:51:43.609 --> 00:51:47.859
In this apparatus, the drug can be made eventually
to hit every cell

00:51:47.859 --> 00:51:49.390
in a cancer mass,

00:51:49.390 --> 00:51:54.600
no matter when or how long it is susceptible
to the drug.

00:51:54.600 --> 00:51:57.290
But scientists thought it might be better
to treat the animal

00:51:57.290 --> 00:51:59.940
with a single, stronger dose,

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:02.650
followed by other doses at a later time,

00:52:02.650 --> 00:52:05.420
perhaps giving the normal cells more time
to recover

00:52:05.420 --> 00:52:09.730
from drug damage before they are treated again.

00:52:09.730 --> 00:52:14.380
Not long ago, Southern Research Institute
tested cytosine arabinoside

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:16.990
in a batch of mice with leukemia.

00:52:16.990 --> 00:52:23.030
[A researcher makes notes on a pad of paper.]

00:52:25.220 --> 00:52:26.780
In the first experiment,

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:30.240
they gave a measured dose of drug to hundreds
of animals,

00:52:30.240 --> 00:52:31.550
once each day for a period

00:52:31.550 --> 00:52:33.740
of fifteen days.

00:52:33.740 --> 00:52:48.640
Some mice were used as controls; they got no drug.

00:52:53.619 --> 00:52:54.960
At the end of the experiment,

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:56.680
the control mice died,

00:52:56.680 --> 00:53:06.580
as predicted on the basis of the growing knowledge
of cell kinetics.

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:23.280
The experimental mice, those that got the drug,  lived much longer, although all eventually died.

00:53:23.360 --> 00:53:26.660
When the results were averaged out, it was
determined that

00:53:26.660 --> 00:53:30.040
the daily dosage had increased the lifespan of the

00:53:30.080 --> 00:53:35.260
experimental mice substantially, although none were cured.

00:53:35.980 --> 00:53:40.240
But, by simply changing the timing of the
administration,

00:53:40.250 --> 00:53:44.300
the scientists got dramatically different
results.

00:53:44.300 --> 00:53:46.420
They used the same kind of mice,

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:49.119
mice which have been inbred for generations

00:53:49.119 --> 00:53:53.230
to enhance their similarity for laboratory purposes.

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:57.450
And the same drug, cytosine arabinoside,

00:53:57.450 --> 00:54:01.530
and essentially the same size dose.

00:54:01.530 --> 00:54:04.320
But instead of administering the drug once
a day,

00:54:04.320 --> 00:54:08.220
they administered it every three hours around
the clock,

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:15.700
at four-day intervals.

00:54:18.130 --> 00:54:19.940
Control mice again got no drug,

00:54:19.940 --> 00:54:27.760
and again they died according to estimates
based on cell kinetics.

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:34.460
The experimental mice, those that got the
drug on the new schedule,

00:54:34.460 --> 00:54:42.820
continued to live happily.

00:54:47.710 --> 00:54:51.500
Some laboratory animals can be cured of cancer,

00:54:51.500 --> 00:54:56.320
but human beings are a far cry from laboratory
animals.

00:54:56.320 --> 00:55:01.570
Can drugs be found or designed that will destroy
the billions of cancer cells

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:05.440
that sometimes spread through human beings?

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:11.840
[A person enters a number using a rotary dialpad
on a large computer.]

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:18.400
A computer program developed at the National
Cancer Institute may help find the answer.

00:55:18.400 --> 00:55:20.290
Into the computer are fed assumptions

00:55:20.290 --> 00:55:24.550
about the generation time of a given cancer cell,

00:55:24.550 --> 00:55:27.530
the duration of the S phase,

00:55:27.530 --> 00:55:31.859
and the fractional cell kill of a given drug.

00:55:31.859 --> 00:55:35.330
In seconds, the computer can reveal, among
other things,

00:55:35.330 --> 00:55:39.480
the number of cells remaining at the end of
the dose period.

00:55:39.480 --> 00:55:47.119
With such information, drug programs can be
tailored for individual patients.

00:55:47.119 --> 00:55:49.560
Other progress is being made.

00:55:49.560 --> 00:55:52.869
The animal model for testing drug activity
against cancer

00:55:52.869 --> 00:55:55.970
is giving good biological information

00:55:55.970 --> 00:56:00.090
that has a direct bearing on the disease in
man.

00:56:00.090 --> 00:56:02.340
Although enormous problems remain,

00:56:02.340 --> 00:56:04.200
there is hope that continued research

00:56:04.200 --> 00:56:09.420
will lead to even greater life expectancy
for even more patients.

00:56:12.340 --> 00:57:14.520
[Music]

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:20.260
[Narrator:]  For further information on Cancer chemotherapy, write for a free booklet,

00:57:20.380 --> 00:57:25.180
Drugs versus Cancer, available from the Research Information Branch,

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:30.300
National Cancer Institute, Bethesda, Maryland.

